WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.810
Canada's official motto translates as
"from sea to sea."

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.940
But for many years now, there's been
an unofficial version:

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.340
"from sea to sea to sea."

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.830
Because Canada doesn't just
have west and east coasts,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.160
it has a north one as well.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.880
Just over that hill is Inuvik,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.880
a small town of about 3,000 people
in the Northwest Territories,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.630
about 200 kilometres
north of the Arctic Circle.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.700
But it's not on the Arctic Ocean.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.800
Until recently, this was the
end of the Dempster Highway,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.110
about as far north as you could
get by Canada's road system.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.130
There wasn't a continuous link
from sea to sea to sea.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.220
Except in winter when the ice was
frozen enough to support vehicles.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.140
But in 2017, after years of
difficult construction over the permafrost

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.620
and hundreds of millions
of dollars in costs,

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.770
an all-weather highway finally opened,
from here to Tuktoyaktuk on the Arctic Ocean.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.020
So for the first time, we can drive there.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.592
...or we could take a plane.

00:00:51.592 --> 00:00:53.020
RADIO: "ATC clear."

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.880
It turns out that building an
all-weather highway across permafrost

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.460
is an incredibly difficult
logistical challenge.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.550
It's now springtime.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.850
The thaw is beginning and
large sections of the highway,

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.400
particularly in the south,
have pretty much turned to mud.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.620
There isn't quite enough gravel,

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.650
it isn't compact enough,
it isn't cold enough.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.450
If your vehicle gets caught there,
you are hours from help

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.380
and you have no cell phone service.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.220
So for safety, the government
has closed the road, at least for now.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.040
But sooner or later,
the road will be fixed

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.860
and the highway will reopen

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.400
and Tuktoyaktuk will be connected again.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.600
And that's going to change things here.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.240
- I was born and raised here.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.460
21 years ago(!)
- [laughter]

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.320
- But anyways, I lived here all my life.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.200
Never going to move.
I enjoy my life here.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.840
There's just under 1,000 people
that live here year-round.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.400
We do a lot of fishing, lot of hunting,
berry picking, whatnot.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.460
Do the seasonal thing.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.400
When they got the go-ahead to finally
start building the road,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.840
it took about five years and they
would only work in the wintertime

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.120
because the tundra is frozen.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.800
And they don't want to damage the tundra
because once the permafrost's at surface,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.810
it's gonna start melting.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.240
They would make ice bridges.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.580
Even to where they would
haul the gravel to us

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.565
just so they don't do damage.

00:01:58.565 --> 00:02:02.560
Tuktoyaktuk is not isolated anymore.
It's supposed to be year-round.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.440
So if you want to go travel down south,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.390
you just hop on a vehicle,
if you own a vehicle,

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.860
just head down.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.300
Whereas in summer months,
we'd be isolated.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.280
We'd wait for the
scheduled flights to come in

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.060
or we go by boat,
if you own a boat.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.640
Most people were for it.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.120
They were hoping the prices would go down

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.670
for groceries, gas, whatnot.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.390
And the gas price did go down a bit

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.210
because we had a second
gas station open up.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.400
So that is one of the good things.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.720
It just gives more
opportunity for everybody.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:28.660
- Now I want to be clear.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.610
This is not some abandoned Arctic outpost.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:32.800
There's cell phone service here.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.750
There's a lot of people
and there's fast internet.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.400
Eileen, who I interviewed,
she runs the local taxi service.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.150
This is a proper town.
It's just very far north.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.200
The future of the Canadian Arctic
is unknown right now.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.640
Climate change means that
the permafrost is melting.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.550
Sea level rise is already
threatening this town.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.660
This highway is just going to be
one more change in a century of them.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.490
- We never did feel isolated
because we're from here.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.460
Yeah, we're just used to the way
we're living all the time.

